{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And a man who takes his sister.</b> Scripture mentions \"taking\" in the case of his sister even though she has no kiddushin (formal betrothal), because the brother and his sister dwell together in one household, and when his desire overpowers him he takes her and draws her to himself, and he does not need to come to her in the manner of coming to a zonah. And so is the way of Scripture to mention \"taking\" in all those who are secluded together, for \"a woman and her mother\" (Leviticus 20:14), \"her daughter-in-law and her daughter-in-law's daughter\" (Leviticus 18:17), \"a woman and her sister\" (ibid. v. 18), and \"his brother's wife\" (Leviticus 20:21) — all of them are with him in the household. And so, \"A man shall not take his father's wife\" (Deuteronomy 23:1). And it says, \"and he sees her nakedness,\" by way of admonition, for Scripture uses euphemisms for forbidden relations: sometimes it calls the act of intercourse \"uncovering nakedness,\" as it says in most cases, \"you shall not uncover her nakedness,\" and this is the practice of zonim who uncover her skirts, as it says, \"and I will uncover your skirts upon your face\" (Nahum 3:5); and sometimes it designates it by the term ביאה (coming): \"and comes to her and hates her\" (Deuteronomy 22:13), \"to come upon us\" (Genesis 19:31), \"and he came to her and she conceived for him\" (ibid. 38:18); and many times it calls it שכיבה (lying); and here it designates it by ראיה (seeing), for the brother and his sister sleep together and there is no need to uncover skirts; and it also designates it by ידיעה (knowing), in the language of \"and the man knew Eve his wife\" (ibid. 4:1), \"and he did not again know her\" (ibid. 38:26), \"a virgin whom no man had known\" (ibid. 24:16). And it says, \"and she sees his nakedness,\" that she too desired his nakedness in her heart and wanted to do likewise. And it mentions this in the case of a sister alone, because with all the forbidden relations, when the man approaches to uncover her nakedness, it is with her consent, for if it is not with her consent she would distance herself from him and cry out; but with the brother who sleeps with her, perhaps without her knowledge he will engage in relations with her. Therefore it mentions that she too sees his nakedness." "And the meaning of <b>it is chesed</b> according to the commentators is: disgrace, for all people by their nature feel shame in this ugly sin. And it is like the Aramaic language, for Onkelos translates \"for it is a disgrace to us\" (Genesis 34:14) — ארי חיסודא היא, and Jonathan translates \"and I will make her a disgrace\" (I Samuel 11:2) — חיסודא, and in the language of the Sages (Ruth Rabbah 7:11): because he acted with chesed in public. And so, \"lest the hearer shame you\" (Proverbs 25:10) — he will make you a disgrace when you reveal another's secret. And the meaning of \"and they shall be cut off in the sight of the children of their people\" — to say, you do this in secret and HaShem will expose your sin by bringing punishment upon you in the sight of all the children of your people. And Scripture mentions likewise in the case of a sin done in the innermost chambers, and the same applies to all karet punishments as I have mentioned (Ramban on Leviticus 18:29). And the meaning of \"he has uncovered his sister's nakedness, he shall bear his iniquity\" — and if he uncovered his sister's nakedness by force, he alone shall bear his iniquity, according to R. Abraham ibn Ezra (on Leviticus 20:17). And in my opinion its meaning is that \"he has uncovered his sister's nakedness, he shall bear his iniquity\" applies to each one of those mentioned, like \"for he has uncovered the flesh of his near kin, they shall bear their iniquity\" (Leviticus 20:19). And the correct interpretation in my eyes for the word חֶסֶד is its plain meaning, and so is the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifra ad loc. and Sanhedrin 58a): he says that the flesh-relationship of brothers is chesed, and they are not fit for uncovering of nakedness, for with other close relatives he mentions the reason of the flesh-relationship, but with brothers he mentions the chesed that is between them, and the word אִישׁ would draw itself and another with it. Or it is in the manner of \"but I am prayer\" (Psalms 109:4), \"for you are coveted\" (Daniel 9:23), \"here I am against you, O insolent one\" (Jeremiah 50:31), from which the word אִישׁ is omitted, or it describes the man with an epithet — it says: and a man who takes his sister and sees her nakedness, he is a man of chesed, and they shall be cut off, for he has uncovered his sister's nakedness, he shall bear his iniquity. It mentions that he should have been a doer of kindness for himself, a man of chesed, yet he corrupts his flesh-kin, acting cruelly, for he was obligated to do for her the chesed that brothers do — to give her in marriage to a husband — but he disqualifies and corrupts her. And Scripture imputes the foulness to the male, like \"he has uncovered his brother's nakedness, they shall remain childless\" (Leviticus 20:21). And so, in my opinion, \"lest the hearer shame you\" (Proverbs 25:10) means: he will remove from you all chesed, because you did not keep your chesed toward your fellow who entrusted his secret to you, like the word לְדַשְּׁנוֹ (Exodus 27:3), וּבְכָל תְּבוּאָתִי תְּשָׁרֵשׁ (Job 31:12) and similar cases, for it is far from me that the word חֶסֶד in the Holy Language should be used in these inverted senses, while the verses praise and pray using the word חֶסֶד. But חיסודא in Aramaic is a different tongue, and even that language distinguishes between them: one is translated חסדא and the other חיסודא. And Rabbeinu Chananel wrote that \"the kindness of nations is sin\" (Proverbs 14:34) means disgrace. And in my opinion this too is an inverted usage, for צְדָקָה and חֶסֶד are mentioned in that verse, and they are the twin brothers mentioned everywhere, like \"he who pursues righteousness and kindness\" (ibid. 21:21), \"I am YHWH who does kindness, justice, and righteousness in the earth\" (Jeremiah 9:23). But the plain sense of the verse according to my opinion is: righteousness exalts a nation when it does it, and the kindness of nations is sin when they do not do it — he says that by righteousness and kindness every nation is either exalted or caused to stumble. Or he says: righteousness exalts the particular nation that does it, while many nations sin regarding kindness and do not perform it. And the parallel to this verse is the one adjacent to it (Proverbs 14:33): \"wisdom rests in the heart of the understanding, but among fools it is made known\" — whose meaning is that it is made known that it is not in their midst, for all who see them will recognize from their deeds that they are fools and have no understanding — and both of these are framed in the same structure of contrast."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The verse citations in this comment have been normalized to standard chapter:verse format; Ramban cites them by a mix of verse-number and locative conventions.", 1 "Ramban here discusses at length whether חֶסֶד in Leviticus 20:17 carries an inverted meaning ('disgrace') or its plain sense ('kindness'); he rejects the inverted reading. The citation of Sifra and Sanhedrin 58a is given parenthetically in the Hebrew and rendered here parenthetically."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:33.382612807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}